Fixed: /cd back into the launch directory no longer warns that its own .yoyo.toml is "NOT applied". It's committed as d55e90e3, whose title carries Fixes #1032.

What I did
• The change: this is the fix that was reverted last session (#1035) only because it was bundled with a protected-file edit. I re-applied that saved patch with skills/ left out; nothing under skills/ is in the commit.
  • config.rs now records the loaded config's full path at startup.
  • cd_config_note.rs checks the /cd target's .yoyo.toml against that path. If it's the loaded file, the "NOT applied" list is dropped. If the check fails for any reason, the old warning still prints.
• Size register: config.rs grew 20 lines (4742 → 4762). The module-size test asked for its register entry to be updated, so I updated it in tests/module_size.rs.
• Docs: I updated docs/src/configuration/permissions.md and the ARCHITECTURE.md entry (corrected its timestamp, added a note on the revert and retry).

Verification
• cargo fmt --check, cargo build and clippy with -D warnings are clean, and cargo test passes across all test binaries.
• Positive control: I forced is_loaded_config to always say "not the loaded file" and ran the tests. cd_back_into_launch_dir_does_not_call_loaded_config_unapplied failed by name. After I restored it, all 32 tests in the module passed.
• Real binary: I started in A (its .yoyo.toml sets a hook) with --trust-project, then ran /cd ../B and /cd ../A.
  • The current main binary prints ⚠ warning: /tmp/p1032/A/.yoyo.toml sets hooks and they are NOT applied… on returning to A.
  • The fixed binary prints no such line, only "hook … fires again".
  • /cd into C, which has its own hooks, still prints the full warning, so other directories still get the warning. The raw output is in the commit body.

Left on #869
I ran the leftover case and posted the output on #869 (confirmed it shows in gh issue view 869 --comments). If the launch file also has [permissions] deny entries, going home still prints the deny note. That note ends "allow, hooks and MCP servers would still not be reloaded on /cd". Each clause is true, but it reads as if something is missing for a file that's already in force. Those deny entries also get added to the list a second time. I didn't check whether /permissions then shows them twice.
